# Force LF line endings for source files regardless of the platform
# Git client's autocrlf default. Without this, Windows CI checks out
# Rust files with CRLF and `rustfmt --check` fails on every one with
# "Incorrect newline style". Frontend formatters tolerate either, but
# pinning them keeps diffs consistent across platforms.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
# Fixtures and sources that tests read byte for byte, such as the IPC
# names list, must keep LF on a Windows checkout too.
*.txt text eol=lf
*.gmcp text eol=lf
*.lua text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
# Shaders reach the GPU byte for byte through include_str!.
*.wgsl text eol=lf

# Binary file types — never touched by line-ending normalization.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
# Wire fixtures hold raw telnet bytes with \n\r line ends that a checkout
# must never touch.
*.bin binary
# The saved log goldens hold a file byte for byte, escape codes and all.
fixtures/saved-logs/** -text
